# Controllers design

Controllers holds the Fearless manager: a desktop program that keeps a set of project folders, compiles them, runs their mains, and connects them to the desktop and to various IDEs, for now just Eclipse.
Next to it, and independent from it, Controllers also holds:
- `fearlessPluginProject`: the Eclipse plugin showing the projects of a manager inside Eclipse, with the content assist of `suggest`.
- `agentTools`: a library driving the real pointer and keyboard, for GUI tests of any program.
- `benchmarks`: speed measures of Fearless programs against the runtime.

The manager is an island: it behaves the same on every system, and this is its objective.
When a part of the system (a tray, a dialog, a desktop shell) would make the manager behave differently than this design, the manager does not use that part that way.
When the system does not offer what the manager needs (a display, a file system feature the manager relies on), the manager stops with a fatal error saying so.
This is the only concept of 'fatal error'. The manager should attempt to discover fatal errors as early as possible, but may discover fatal errors while running. For example, the disk could stop working or the system could start reporting that there is no display.
Thus, the following are not fatal errors: calling the manager program with wrong arguments (a wrong format, or a path to a folder that does not exist).
The problems of one project (bad file names, a bad icon, a missing folder, a failing compile) are not fatal errors, they are part of normally running the manager.

A fatal error is shown in a dialog listing the jobs that are running.
A fatal error ends the manager. This happens in order:
-The dialog shows. Nothing below happens until the dialog has been closed.
-All the running jobs are terminated. The manager waits until every job has terminated.
-The manager deletes the manager folder.
-The manager removes all the associations the manager took.

Everything the manager is asked to do either happens or is refused with a message naming what was refused and why; nothing is silently skipped.

## Program and manager folder

A manager is one program folder, `fearlessManaged<version>`, for example `fearlessManaged0_001`.
Its manager folder is `fearless<version>`, next to the program folder, on every system. The manager folder holds everything the manager remembers; deleting the manager folder makes the manager start from nothing. A manager starting with no manager folder removes the icons and the file associations the manager took.
A manager folder that can not be created is a fatal error naming it.

## Starting the 'single' manager.
Starting the manager program leaves one message in the manager folder. Several runs of the manager program can be alive at the same time, and one of them is the 'single' manager: the run of the manager program handling the messages.

The manager program takes:
- zero arguments: the message is `Show`: show the window, starting the single manager when none runs;
- a first argument `FearlessCommand<Verb>`, then the arguments of the verb: the message is the verb `<Verb>` with those arguments. A project name, a main, a kind and a type name are written as they are. A path is written as a tagged text in its `Base16:` form, `Base16:` then the bytes naming the path, so every argument is plain ASCII on every system. A port is written `Port:` then its number. The words after the project name of `Run` and `Link` form their second argument, separated by single spaces, except a last `Port:` word, the port;
- one argument not starting with `FearlessCommand`: the path of a file or a folder, as the system passes the path when the file is opened (for example by a double click); the message is `Register` with that path for a folder or a `.fearless` file, and `OpenWithOrShortcut` with that path for any other file.
For example:
```
FearlessCommandQuit
FearlessCommandShow hello_world
FearlessCommandRun hello_world hello.Hello1
FearlessCommandRun hello_world hello.Hello1 hello.Hello3 Port:51234
FearlessCommandRegister Base16:2F646174612F68656C6C6F
```
(the last one registers `/data/hello` on Linux).
A first argument starting with `FearlessCommand` is always a verb, never a path.
Every run of the manager program writes its message file. A run that is not the single manager then ends; a run that becomes the single manager applies its own message file like every other message file.
A run of the manager program called with bad arguments leaves the message `InvalidCall` with the details. When the single manager handles `InvalidCall`, the single manager shows the error in a dialog and continues running.
A run of the manager program encountering a fatal error while the single manager is running, or is likely to start soon, leaves the message `FatalError` with the details and ends normally. When possible, the single manager is the only thing taking actual actions.
The single manager handles `FatalError` as a fatal error.
A run of the manager program encountering a fatal error likely to prevent a single manager from starting deletes the manager folder and removes all the associations the manager took. By definition there are no running jobs to terminate in this case.

The single manager runs until Quit, or until a fatal error. Quit ends the manager and every job the manager runs.

Closing the manager window hides the manager window; an icon stays visible to show that the single manager is still running.
On a system with a tray (a system tray or StatusNotifierItem), the icon is a tray icon.
Clicking the tray icon, with any button, opens the menu of the tray icon: Show manager and Quit manager.

On a system with no tray, the manager shows the icon itself.
The manager window is closed and replaced with the 'tray icon window': a window of the size of a tray icon, with no title bar and no border, holding only the Fearless icon, in the bottom right corner of the screen and always above the other windows.
Clicking the tray icon window, with any button, opens the menu of the tray icon.
For half a second after that menu shows, a press or a release on its entries does nothing.
An entry of that menu is chosen by a click: a press and a release of a button on that same entry.
Showing the manager removes the tray icon window and shows the manager window.

## Requests and messages

Everything the manager does for a project is a request, and every request is one of the requests below, whether it comes from a message or from a GUI operation.
The single manager applies the requests one at a time, in the order they arrive, each one completely before the next.

A message is the file `messages/<time>-<id>.msg` in the manager folder: `<time>` is the 20 digit count of milliseconds since 1970 when the message was written, and `<id>` is a random UUID. The single manager applies the message files in the order of their names, and deletes each message file after reading it.
Only the manager program writes message files: the manager program writes `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp`, then renames `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp` to `messages/<time>-<id>.msg`, so the single manager never reads a partial message. The single manager deletes every `.tmp` file older than 60 seconds.
The single manager relies on the system telling the single manager when a file appears in `messages`. When a run of the manager program becomes the single manager, the single manager writes a `.tmp` file in `messages` and waits for that report; no report within 5 seconds is a fatal error. Some file systems, for example some network drives, do not report new files.
Every other program commanding the manager (the Eclipse plugin, a script, a test) runs the manager program with the arguments `FearlessCommand<Verb>` and the arguments of the verb.
GUI operations command the manager the same way, through `FearlessCommand<Verb>` and the arguments of the verb:
every GUI operation has an equivalent message, and every message is either ill formed or has an equivalent GUI operation.
A request changes files (those of the manager folder, those in the `.fearless_out` of a project, or the Fearless plugin of an Eclipse), or starts or ends jobs, or is one of the two requests about the manager itself: `Show` and `Quit`.
The GUI operations that do none of this are not requests and have no message: Open docs, Base documentation, Browse and Logs, which open another program on a file, and the Clear button of an Output, which empties the Output. A GUI operation that finds nothing to open says so in a dialog.
Since all errors are caused by messages and all messages go via the single manager, a program that sends a message gets the error, if any, in the manager window. A hidden manager window is shown first.
Showing the manager window asks the desktop to bring the manager window to the front. A desktop that keeps windows from taking the focus, such as GNOME, may leave the manager window behind the window the user is working in; such a desktop tells the user in its own way, for example with a notification that Fearless Manager is ready.

A message is a verb, then the arguments of the verb, one per line.
A verb is one of the following, written in upper camel case. Verbs about the whole manager:
- `Show`: show the window; with the name of a registered project as its argument, also select that project;
- `Register <path>`, the path as a tagged text: register the folder, or the folder a file is in, then select it; a folder already registered is only selected;
- `Quit`: end the manager and every job it runs;
- `Reset`: end the manager and every job it runs, remove every association the manager took, and delete the manager folder;
- `ConnectEclipse <path>`, the path as a tagged text: connect the Eclipse found from the path, then say so in a dialog, asking to restart Eclipse if it is running and to open the Fearless perspective;
- `EditMetadata <path>`, the path as a tagged text: replace `projects.info` with the content of the file at the path;
- `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>`, the path as a tagged text: open the file with the main whose `OpenWith` or `Shortcut` gives its extension. Double clicking the file on the desktop is its gui operation.
- `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`: open the file with the main `<main>` of the project `<name>`, one of the mains claiming the extension of the file. When several mains claim that extension, `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>` shows a window listing them (see Opening a file); picking a main in that window is its gui operation, with the project and the main of the picked entry. Another program, for example an agent, sends it to choose the main itself.
Verbs about one project, taking the name of a registered project:
- `Run <name>`: select the project and run the mains of the second argument, separated by spaces, in the order of the mains of the project; they become the selected mains: the last mains started take the lead;
- `Compile <name>`: select the project and compile it if it needs compiling;
- `Check <name>`: select the project and report in its Output why it is invalid or out of date, or that it is neither; why it is invalid is also written to `.fearless_out/check_error.txt`, and a `Check` of a valid project deletes `.fearless_out/check_error.txt`;
- `Terminate <name>`: select the project and end its job, if any;
- `DeleteCache <name>`: select the project and delete its compiled cache;
- `Kind <name>`: set the kind of the project to the second argument (`idle`, `code`, `data:readOnly`, `data:readWrite`);
- `Forget <name>`: forget the project.
- `Link <name>`: set a link of the code project to a data project to the second argument: the name of the data project, then `read` or `write`, then the type names the code uses for it, separated by spaces; no type names removes that link;
Every verb takes a port as its last argument, when present.
Before applying the request, the manager connects to that port of this machine.
What the manager sends on the connection starts with a line saying what became of the message: `Applied` or `Refused`.
After `Refused`, the manager sends why, as the dialog shows it, and ends the connection.
After `Applied`, the manager ends the connection at once, except for `Run` and `Compile`.
For `Run` and `Compile`, the connection is where the Output of the job goes, also: after `Applied`, the manager sends there what the job prints, from its start and then as it prints it, with its marks of where the job and each main start and end; the connection ends with the job, at once when there is no job.
`Quit` and `Reset` may end the manager before it sends `Applied`.
This is how another program (Eclipse, a running main) knows that the manager has applied its message, and follows a job it asked for.
Two more message files exist, `InvalidCall <details>` and `FatalError <details>`, written only by a run of the manager program that is not the single manager, about its own failure. They are not verbs: no argument of the manager program and no gui operation produces them, and the argument `FearlessCommandInvalidCall` or `FearlessCommandFatalError` is an invalid call.
The details of `InvalidCall` and `FatalError` are every line after the verb, any number of lines.

Ill formed messages:
- a message with a number of lines its verb does not take;
- a port where no program of this machine accepts the connection;
- a verb that is not one of these;
- a name that is not the name of a registered project (the message lists the registered projects);
- a `Register` whose path is blank, names nothing, is the root of a drive, or is the manager folder, anything inside it or a folder holding it;
- a `ConnectEclipse` whose path leads to no Eclipse folder, and an `EditMetadata` whose path names no file;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut` whose path names no file, names an empty file, or names a file whose extension no main claims;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>` where `<main>` of `<name>` is not one of the mains claiming the extension of the file, also when `<name>` is not a registered project (the message lists the mains claiming it);
- a kind that is not one of the four, and a malformed link;
- a request whose result would make `projects.info` invalid (the message is the error of `projects.info`).
- `Run` and `Compile` of a project that is not code, naming its kind;
- `Run` and `Compile` of a project with invalid content, saying why (for a broken link, naming the link and why);
- `Run` of a project that is not compiled, naming its state; `Run` naming no main, or a main that is not a main of the project (the message lists the mains);
- `Link` of a project that is not code;
- a job, `DeleteCache`, `Kind` or `Forget` while the project has a job;
- a `Kind` change that is not from idle or back to idle;
- a request changing the data projects that a running main reads or writes (see Data projects of a running main).

Whether a message is ill formed is decided when it arrives, from the message and the current state, before anything is compiled, run or written to the Output.
An ill formed message is shown in a dialog, never in the Output: the Output of a project holds only the results of well formed requests. A failing compile is such a result: the request is right, the code is broken.

The window's GUI elements should be designed with good GUI design with the principle of 'discoverability' in mind.
This includes both display elements and buttons and menus allowing to send the messages described above.

## Info files

The files the manager and the compiler write for the manager and for Eclipse (`projects.info`, `manager.info`, `mains.info`) are Info files: text in the Fearless Str allowed character set.
The Info format is a simplified Json format. 
Infos are strings `"..."`, lists `[a, b]`, or objects `{"key": value, ...}` whose keys are distinct strings;
Comments are not supported.
Inside an info string, `\"` is a quote, `\\` a backslash and `\n` a newline; no other escape are present; thus `"\t"` would be an invalid Info.

A text that may hold characters outside the Fearless character set is a tagged text:
- `"Str:.."` then the text, when every character is in the Fearless character set: `"path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\hello\""`;
We use this when the string is a valid Fearless Str. Note `\\` instead of `\\\\` in the example above. The fearless Str is `C:\data\hello` and prints as `"C:\data\hello"` (fearless code that would produce that same string content when parsed). This works since fearless strings do not use `\` for escapes at all.
For example the string `Hello\nWorld` is produced from the fearless code `"Hello"|"World"` and thus it would be encoded as `"Str:\"Hello\"|\"World\""`
- `"UStr:.."` then the text as `UStr.escape` prints it. We use this when the string would not be a valid Str but it would be a valid UStr. Refer to UStr.escape to understand this format.
- `"Base16:.."` then the bytes. This is needed, for example, to represent arbitrary file system paths that may not be valid unicode. This encoding should faithfully represent the bytes that the current file system would be able to interpret as the specific file path.


## Registered projects

The manager folder holds `projects.info`, the record of every registered project: where it is, its kind, its selected mains, and what the project may read and write.
The manager is the only program writing `projects.info`: it writes the new content to another file in the manager folder, then renames that file to `projects.info`, so a reader such as Eclipse never reads a partial `projects.info`.

`projects.info` is an Info file: an object with `projects`, mapping each project name to its metadata, and `systemExtensions`:
```
{
  "projects": {
    "hello_world": {
      "path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\01_hello_world\"",
      "kind": "code",
      "mains": ["hello.Hello1", "hello.Hello3"],
      "reads": {"pub": ["Pub"], "notes": ["OldNotes"]},
      "edits": {"notes": ["Notes", "Diary"]}
    },
    "diary": {
      "path": "UStr:\"C:\\Users\\Jos\".u\"00E9\"+(\"\\da\".u\"1F600\")+(\"ta\".u)",
      "kind": "data:readWrite"
    }
  },
  "systemExtensions": ["htm"]
}
```
- `systemExtensions`: the system extensions this manager captures, whether or not a main opens them. A system extension is an extension a main names in its `OpenWith`, like `htm` in `base.OpenWith[icon,"htm"]`; every other `OpenWith` and `Shortcut` gets an extension the manager makes (`ffile000` to `ffile999`, `fapp000` to `fapp999`). A compile whose mains name a system extension missing from `systemExtensions` fails, naming the extension.
Each project has:
- `path`: the absolute path of the project folder, any path the system allows, exactly as the current system and file system name it, separators included: the tagged text only picks the most readable form holding those exact bytes. Two projects never share a folder, and a project folder is never inside another one.
- `kind`: `idle`, `code`, `data:readOnly` or `data:readWrite`.
- `mains`: the selected mains: the mains the last `Run` ran, for a code project with several mains.
- `reads` and `edits`: for a code project, the data projects it may read, and those it may read and write, each with the type names the code uses for it. A project in `reads` is `data:readOnly` or `data:readWrite`; a project in `edits` is `data:readWrite`. A data project may be in both: the type names in `edits` read and write, those in `reads` only read. A type name is in one link only: the code reaches one data project through it. A link names at least one type name.

A code project whose links name a missing project, or a project of the wrong kind, or an invalid project, is invalid, and the message says which link and why.

A `projects.info` that is not a valid Info file, or does not hold what is above, is a fatal error pointing at the offending text in the Frontend error message style. 
A manager initiated action should never edit a project info file in a way that is invalid, thus the fatal error above shows either a manager bug or OS/user interference in the manager folder.

The whole file can be edited by hand from the GUI, or via a message naming a file (outside of the manager folder) with the intended new content.
The editor of the GUI remembers the content it opened; when the user commits, and `projects.info` no longer holds that content, the editor refuses the commit in a dialog, and the user edits the new content again; otherwise the commit is an `EditMetadata`.
An edit is accepted only if the whole text is valid, and an error points at the offending text in the Frontend error message style.
An accepted edit applies at once: projects it removes are forgotten, projects it adds are registered as they are, every project is checked again.



## Registering a folder

The name of a project is the name of its marker: the only file with the `.fearless` extension at the top of its folder, `<name>.fearless`. A project name follows the rules of a single file name segment of a Fearless project (lowercase letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit, not a reserved name such as `con`).

Registering a new folder that is not registered already:
Error if: the folder is inside an already registered one, or an already registered project is inside the folder.
Name inference: The name is the name of its marker, when it has exactly one, the name is valid, and no other project uses it.
Otherwise the name is the folder name turned into a valid name: lowercased, every run of other characters turned into `_`, a leading `p` when it starts with a digit, then the first of `name`, `name2`, `name3`... not used by another project. The marker is renamed (or created) to match.
If present, the compiled cache (`.fearless_out`) of the newly registered folder is deleted.
`.fearless_out` belongs to the manager in every registered folder, whatever its kind: `data: read only` means that the Fearless programs do not edit the data, not that the manager does not write in its folder.
The folder is now in the 'idle' state.

Forgetting a project asks no confirmation.
Forgetting a project removes the project from `projects.info`; nothing in the project folder is changed.

## Project states

Each project is in one of these states, shown as a badge on its tile:
- `code: invalid content`, `data: invalid content`: its folder is missing, its file names are not valid Fearless file names (for code, also its package structure), its top level .fearless file is missing or not the only one, dangling links to data projects this project uses, or the manager can not display this project icon;
- `idle`;
- `data: read only`, `data: read write`;
- `code: not compiled (no cache)`: no package is compiled, even if `.fearless_out` exists and holds the error of the last check or compile;
- `code: not compiled (cache out of date)`: a compiled cache that does not match the sources;
- `code: compiled`;
- `code: busy`: a job runs.

A code project is out of date exactly when compiling it would compile some package: when the compiled cache of a package does not list exactly the current files of that package, or is older than one of them. Files outside every package (a readme, the marker) never make a project out of date.

The mains of a code project are known when it is not out of date: the concrete mains of the highest rank (could be more than one package).
When reading them finds a package that compiling would compile, or reading them fails, the project state becomes out of date. A project whose files change while it is checked is checked again.

While the window is shown, the manager notices changes made on disk by other programs: every three seconds it checks the selected project, and in turn one other project, and updates states, badges and buttons, including moving a project in and out of invalid. A project with a running job is checked when the job ends. Every request naming a project checks it.
The manager does not check for changes when the window is not shown. This include the tray, true or emulated, and minimization.

The icon of a project is the only PNG file in the folder `.config/icon` of the project, or, with no such folder, an icon generated from the project name. More than one PNG, or a PNG that can not be read, makes the project invalid.

Kinds change request: from idle to any kind, from any kind back to idle. Changing kind keeps `mains`, `reads` and `edits`: they have no effect while the project is not code, and come back if it becomes code again.

## General Gui shape:
Dropping files or folders on the window is `Register` of each of them.

A menu bar at the top, with the Manager menu: Add folder (`Register`, through a file picker), Edit project metadata (`EditMetadata`), Connect Eclipse (`ConnectEclipse`), Base documentation, Reset (`Reset`, after a confirmation dialog saying what Reset does, with the buttons Reset and Cancel, Cancel preselected), Quit (`Quit`).

A simplified 'desktop with icons' fills the window under the menu bar while no project is selected; while a project is selected, it takes the left side of a vertical divider, and the project area takes the right side.
Icons represent registered folders, sorted by name: each shows the project icon with its state badge, and the project name under it.
A busy project (compiling or running) shows an animated badge.
Clicking an icon is `Show` with the name of its project.
Double clicking an icon of a project in the state `code: compiled` is `Run` of that project naming its only main, or, for a project with several mains, its selected mains (`mains` in `projects.info`). For any other project, and for a project with several mains and no selected main, a double click is a click.

## Project area contains, top to bottom:

A header on one line: the project icon, the project name, and the following buttons:
- The main button, by the state of the project:
  - Check, for a project that is not code, and for a code project with invalid content;
  - Compile, for a code project that is not compiled;
  - Run, for a compiled code project with one main; Run V, for one with several mains; Run greyed out, for one with no main;
  - Terminate, while the project has a job.
- The Open docs button (enabled once the mains are known). It opens the docs in the favourite browser.
- The kind buttons: Only "Back to idle" for a project that is not idle;
for an idle project "Become data", "Become editable data" and "Become code".
- Browse, opening the project folder in the file explorer, Forget, and for a code project Delete cache (`DeleteCache`).
The kind buttons, Forget and Delete cache are greyed out while the project has a job.

For a code project, if there is more than one main
we see "Run 'V'" where V is some arrow down signalling that there is more than just running.
When Run V is pressed, it behaves like a drop-down menu showing the available mains, with a checkbox near their names, the selected mains checked, and a 'Run' button at the end of the checkbox list, disabled if nothing is checked. That 'Run' button is `Run` naming the checked mains; the Run button of a project with one main is `Run` naming that main.
  
For a code project whose mains have a `Shortcut`, after the header we see each shortcut file as the simplified desktop shows a project: the icon of its `Shortcut`, with the name of the shortcut file under it.
Double clicking on it is `OpenWithOrShortcut` with the path of its shortcut file: the very message a double click on that file on the desktop sends.


For every project, we have an Output collapsible area with vertical scroll bars and max size of 1/2 of the gui height, and a Clear button on its title. What the main button produces always goes there, and the Output keeps all of it until it is cleared.

For a code project whose mains open extensions (`OpenWith`), after the Output we see a collapsible area with vertical scroll bars and max size of 1/2 of the gui height, one block per main opening extensions:
  "Extensions this main opens:" (each extension with its icon),
  "Conflicting extensions:" (each extension another main also opens, naming that main).

For a data project we see a similar collapsible area with vertical scroll bars and max size of 1/2 of the gui height, listing in editable format the code projects that can see this data, how and under what aliases; another code project can be added there.

For a code project, the same area lists in editable format the data projects this project can see, how and under what aliases; another data project can be added there.

The code project holds the link (`reads` and `edits` in `projects.info`), and is the one that becomes invalid when the link is broken: an edit from either side is a `Link` of the code project. As usual, every edit goes via the messages and is validated when received.

For a code project we have Logs; collapsible area with vertical scroll bars and max size of 1/2 of the gui height; showing the log files the mains wrote in .out/logs, with a Logs button opening that folder in the file explorer.


## Jobs

A project does at most one job at a time: compiling, or running its mains.
Every job, and every `Check`, starts with an empty Output area. Everything a job prints goes there, and the Output marks where the job, and each main of a run, starts and ends, and how it ended, so that Eclipse and the user know when it is over.

- Compile (code projects): a code project with broken links is not compiled. The compiler runs in its own JVM; its output goes to the Output. A compile that ends with the mains known removes from the selected mains those that are no longer mains. A compile that fails leaves the project out of date: the packages it did not compile stay out of date, and a compile that fails after compiling the packages (on an icon, an extension or a shortcut file) deletes `mains.info`, so reading the mains fails. A compile that fails writes its error text to `.fearless_out/compile_error.txt`, and a compile that succeeds deletes `.fearless_out/compile_error.txt`.
- Run (code projects): a code project with broken links does not run. When the project is out of date, the main button compiles. A `Run` runs the mains it names, in the order of the mains of the project. Each main runs in its own JVM, one after the other.
- Terminate ends the job: the JVM of the running main or of the compile ends, and no further main of that run starts.

### Data projects of a running main

A running main reads the data projects of its links through `System.dataRead` of the standard library, and reads and writes them through `System.dataReadWrite`.
The roots of `System.dataRead` are the type names in `reads` and in `edits`; the roots of `System.dataReadWrite` are the type names in `edits`: a main names a data project by a root of the capability it uses. A type name is in one link only, so a root names one data project; one data project can still be linked under several type names, also in `reads` under one and in `edits` under another.
When a `Run` starts, the manager gives each main of the run the folder of every data project its links accept, by the type names of those links.
Changes follow the dependencies of the projects:
- what a running main depends on does not change: a `Link`, `Kind`, `Forget` or `EditMetadata` changing what the links of that project give, or the kind or the folder of a data project they name, is refused, naming the project and its running main;
- what a code project that is not running depends on can change: removing a data project it links, or changing its kind or its folder, is accepted, and the code project becomes invalid, naming the broken link.
A data project that changes on disk while a main runs is not checked again: a read or a write that finds the files changed so that it can not be done is a non deterministic error of that main.
A main does not see the marker of a data project or the files and folders whose name starts with "."; a path naming them is not valid.
A write keeps the data project valid: it writes only valid names, never at the place of the marker, and a delete removes the folders and the zip files it leaves empty.

An error file (`compile_error.txt`, `check_error.txt`) is out of date once a file or folder of the project outside `.fearless_out`, or `projects.info`, is modified after it, as the compiled cache of a package is out of date once one of its files is modified after it. An error file that is out of date is shown nowhere: it says why the project was broken, not why it is broken now.


## File associations

On Windows and Linux the single manager makes its launcher the program opening every captured extension, following `FileAssociations.reconcile` of Commons. The captured extensions are `.fearless`, every extension in `systemExtensions`, and the Fearless extensions the mains of the projects open.
- Extending the captured extensions (an `EditMetadata` adding to `systemExtensions`, a compile giving a main a new Fearless extension) is refused when reconcile refuses a new extension: the edit is invalid, or the compile fails, naming the extension and the program holding it, and nothing changes.
- Losing a captured extension to a program outside the Fearless family is a fatal error when the manager can not take it back. At every start the manager takes back all its captured extensions; `.fearless` is captured from the first start.
- Only one member of the Fearless family at a time opens `.fearless`: a starting manager of any version, or a starting portable Fearless, takes `.fearless` together with every extension of the Fearless program that held it. The single manager notices, whether its window is shown or not, when another member of the Fearless family took `.fearless`: it shows a dialog naming that program, and when the dialog is closed it quits. This is not a fatal error: the manager folder stays, and the next start of this manager takes every extension back.

## OpenWith and Shortcut

A main can ask the Fearless manager to make the desktop open it: on a double click on any file with a given extension (`OpenWith`), or on a small file the manager creates for it (`Shortcut`).

```
Foo: Main, OpenWith[IconsFoo, "foo"]{ s -> ... }
Bar: Main, Shortcut[IconsBar, "fapp123"]{ s -> ... }
Baz: Main, OpenWith[IconsBaz]{ s -> ... }
Qux: Main, Shortcut[IconsQux]{ s -> ... }
```

`IconsFoo` and the others are types the compiler generates for image files recognised as autoloaded assets (here `_pkg/icons/foo.png` becomes `IconsFoo`). They can come from any package the main can see, including `base` once `base` offers autoloaded icons.

Informally: `Foo` wants every `.foo` file to show the `IconsFoo` image and to open `Foo` on a double click. `Bar` wants a file `bar.fapp123` in the top level of its project, showing the `IconsBar` image, that starts `Bar` on a double click; that file carries no information, it is only a handle to start the main, and it keeps working when moved anywhere else.

Mechanically `OpenWith` and `Shortcut` are the same: both claim an extension for a main. `Shortcut` additionally creates the shortcut file.

The manager registers every claimed extension to itself. The desktop starts the manager on the double clicked file; the manager looks up which mains claim that extension and runs the right one.

A missing extension is chosen by the manager. In this case, extensions are meant to be hidden or ignored by users, who recognise their files by the icon.

### Base

```
OpenWith[I,E]: {}
OpenWith[I]: {}
Shortcut[I,E]: {}
Shortcut[I]: {}
```

`Shortcut` does not extend `OpenWith`.

A main can implement several of them at once, for example `OpenWith[Icon1,"a"]` and `OpenWith[Icon2,"b"]` to open two extensions; the icons can differ or be the same. Several `Shortcut` produce several shortcut files for the same main. Implementing the same type twice (`Shortcut[I]` inherited along two paths) counts once.

### Well formedness

The compiler checks every compiled literal whose supertypes contain a claim, before generating code, with errors worded like the Frontend errors and pointing at the declaration. They are well formedness rules on the supertype closure, not typing rules.

1. Every declaration whose supertype closure contains `base.OpenWith[_]`, `base.OpenWith[_,_]`, `base.Shortcut[_]` or `base.Shortcut[_,_]` also has `base.Main` in its supertype closure. This goes in the opposite direction to `Sealed`: the implementer, not the implemented type, must carry something. Since the closure is transitive, the declaration blamed is the one that first adds `OpenWith`/`Shortcut` without `Main`, even when it is meant to be mixed into a main later. The four base declarations themselves are exempt.
   Wording:
   `Type "Assoc" implements "base.OpenWith[_,_]".`
   `Only a main can open files: "Assoc" must also implement "base.Main", directly or through one of its supertypes.`
2. The arguments are concrete: the first is a type name with no generic arguments, the second a `base` string literal type. No type variables, so every check below can be done on the declaration itself.
3. The extension literal: the text between the delimiters, with `"txt"` and `` `txt` `` meaning the same extension `txt`. The two are different type names, so the rule compares the text, and a main claiming both `OpenWith[I,"txt"]` and `` OpenWith[I,`txt`] `` is claiming `txt` twice (rule 5).
   The text follows the rule for a single extension segment of a file in a project: 1 to 16 characters, each `a-z` or `0-9`; no leading dot; no multi dot extensions such as `tar.gz` (a project may hold `tar.gz` files, but Windows opens a file by the text after its last dot, so a claim of `tar.gz` would really be a claim of `gz`). `fearless` is reserved. A text breaking this rule is an invalid extension, refused here.
   Every other text is one of two kinds:
   - A Fearless extension: `fapp[0-9][0-9][0-9]` (shortcuts) or `ffile[0-9][0-9][0-9]` (files). The manager owns these outright: it takes them with no question asked.
   - A system extension: any other valid text, `htm`, `pdf`, `notes` alike. Only `OpenWith` can claim one, and only once the user allowed it in `systemExtensions`.
   A `Shortcut` claims only a Fearless extension: its extension, when written, is a `fapp[0-9][0-9][0-9]`. The shortcut file carries no information and lives in the project, so it must never look like a document of another program, and it must never name a kind of file the project reads itself (`bar.fear`, `bar.zip`). An `OpenWith` written with a `fapp[0-9][0-9][0-9]` is refused too: those name shortcut files.
4. The icon is a type generated by the autoloading of an image file: one error covers every other icon (`base.Str`, `base.ImageFile` itself, a hand written `ImageFile`, a `.txt` asset). Whether the image is a usable PNG is checked by the manager after the compile.
5. One main claims each extension at most once, across all its `OpenWith` and `Shortcut`: `Foo: Main, OpenWith[I1,"a"], Shortcut[I2,"a"]` is refused, since one extension has one icon.

Only mains the manager can run take part: the concrete mains of the highest rank of the project. `OpenWith`/`Shortcut` on a main of a lower rank is legal and has no effect.

### What the compiler records (portable and manager alike)

The portable Fearless does nothing with `OpenWith`/`Shortcut`; the compiler is shared, so it records them in either case, and only the manager reads them.

The compiler writes `.fearless_out/mains.info`, an Info file mapping each main to its declaring file, the list of its `Shortcut` claims, then the list of its `OpenWith` claims. A claim is the icon type, where its image is (the `.diskPath`, `.zipSteps` and `.zipEntry` of the asset, relative to the root of the project owning the icon's package, the stdLib folder for `base`), then the extension, `""` when the main wrote none:
```
{
  "hello.Bar": ["_hello/bar.fear", [["hello.IconsShortcut", "_hello/icons/shortcut.png", "", "", ""]], [["hello.IconsDoc", "_hello/icons/doc.png", "", "", "htm"]]],
  "hello.Beer": ["_hello/beer.fear", [], []]
}
```
Each compiled package records its own part in `.fearless_out/<pkg>.mains.info` (same format), a cache like `<pkg>.json`; after every compile, `mains.info` is assembled from the parts of the top rank packages, so a package becoming top rank needs no recompilation. The manager reads only `mains.info`. An icon from another package of the project is resolved from the autoloaded assets of that package.

### What the manager does after a compile

The compiler runs in its own JVM; everything below runs in the manager after the compiler succeeded, because it needs the claims of every other project.

1. Read the claims of the project from its `mains.info`.
2. **Checking the icon.** Each icon must be a PNG file (autoloading also accepts jpg, gif and bmp; they are refused here), square, with a side from 64 to 1024 pixels. A failure is reported as the compile failing, worded like a Frontend error and naming the main, the icon type and the image file:
   `The icon "hello.IconsBar" in "base.Shortcut[hello.IconsBar,\"bar\"]" of main "hello.Bar" is 300x200 pixels (from "_hello/icons/bar.png"): an icon must be a square PNG image with a side from 64 to 1024 pixels.`
   The portable Fearless ignores icons, so it does not run this check: a project with such an icon compiles there and fails only in the manager.
3. Choose the missing extensions.
The manager writes each chosen extension into `mains.info`, in place of `""`.
4. Materialise every icon in `.fearless_out/icons/`: the PNG copied out (it may be inside a zip, and the desktop needs a plain file), and on Windows an `.ico` made from it. Two claims with the same icon type share the files. Every compile also removes from `.fearless_out/icons/` the files of icons no longer claimed.
The name of an icon in `.fearless_out/icons/` follows from the path of its image, so `mains.info` does not record it.

5. Rebuild the in memory map and reconcile.
6. Create the missing shortcut files.

`projects.info` is unchanged: its `mains` field is the user's choice of which mains to run, not a copy of the compiled mains.

### The in memory map

The manager keeps `extension -> list of (project, main, icon)` in memory only. It builds it at start up from the `mains.info` of every registered `code` project, and rebuilds it after every compile, `DeleteCache` (the project's `.fearless_out` is gone, so it claims nothing), forget, and kind change away from `code`. A project whose `mains.info` is missing claims nothing.

### Autoselected extensions

A `Shortcut` without extension gets `fappNNN`, an `OpenWith` without extension gets `ffileNNN`, where `NNN` are three digits.

1. Start from a number from 000 to 999 computed from the project name in `projects.info`, the qualified main name and the icon, the same on every machine. The icon is part of it so that `Shortcut[I1]` and `Shortcut[I2]` of one main start apart.
2. The number is free if no other claim in the in memory map uses that extension; else try the next one, wrapping from 999 to 000.
3. If all 1000 are taken, the compile fails:
   `No free extension is left for "base.Shortcut[hello.IconsBar]" of main "hello.Bar": all the 1000 extensions "fapp000" to "fapp999" are used by the projects of this manager.`
   For an `OpenWith`, the message adds that a system extension can be claimed instead, for example `"base.OpenWith[hello.IconsFoo,\"foo\"]"`.

Which project compiles first can decide who gets a number. This is accepted.

### Shortcut files

For each shortcut claim, a file `<name>.<extension>` in the top level of the project, next to the `.fearless` marker, holding a single new line, like the marker: desktops type an empty file by its emptiness and ignore its extension (GNOME makes it `application/x-zerosize`), so an empty shortcut file would show no icon and open nothing. `<name>` is the simple name of the main turned into a file name, the inverse of the rule autoloading uses to name the type of an image: each uppercase letter becomes `_` and its lowercase, except the first letter of the name, which only becomes lowercase. `FooBar` becomes `foo_bar`, `_Foo` becomes `_foo`, `Foo_1` becomes `foo_1`, `FooBAR` becomes `foo_b_a_r`.

Since every file name in a project is lowercase, `fooBar.png` is not a valid asset name, so `foo_bar` is the only name that autoloading turns into `FooBar`. The inverse does not exist for every type name: type names can end in primes (`Foo'`), and can contain `_` followed by a lowercase letter (`Foo_bar`), which autoloading never produces. Such a main, one whose name turns into a Windows reserved name (`Con`), or two shortcut mains of the project giving the same file name and extension (`a.Bar` and `b.Bar`), fail the compile:
`Main "hello.Foo'" can not have a Shortcut: its name has no matching file name. A main with a Shortcut has a name that can be mapped to a file name like "FooBar" can be mapped to "foo_bar".`

The shortcut file is a visible file of the project: it follows the project file rules, and the project checks see it. Creating it must not make the project read as modified: this is not a problem: the file is at top level and all the source and autoloaded assets must be inside some folder structure. A shortcut file the user deleted is created again by the next compile; one the user moved out of the project stays where it is, and a new one appears.

### Reconciling

Reconcile takes the whole wanted set at once, so after each change of the captured extensions the manager calls it with all of them: `.fearless`, every extension in `systemExtensions` and every claimed Fearless extension, each with its icon. An extension claimed by exactly one main uses its icon; every other captured extension but `.fearless` uses the conflict icon: one claimed by several mains, and one of `systemExtensions` that no main claims. Reconciling an unchanged set changes nothing; reconcile also runs at manager start up.

The checks of `reconcile` change nothing when they fail, so the previous associations stay in place. A failing `reconcile` after a compile makes that compile fail, and the in memory map goes back to the previous `mains.info` of that project (the manager keeps it until reconcile succeeded). The error names the failing extension, the main claiming it and the program holding it.

The manager settles conflicts among the Fearless apps it manages; with the OS and every other program it needs a clear yes or no, and the two kinds of extension get different answers.
- `.fearless` belongs to the Fearless family alone. A program outside the Fearless family opening it, or the user's own choice of a program for it (a Windows UserChoice, even when it names this manager, or a Linux `[Default Applications]` entry), refuses it. Only one Fearless program at a time opens Fearless extensions: taking `.fearless` replaces the Fearless program that had it (another manager, the portable Fearless).
- A Fearless extension (`fappNNN`, `ffileNNN`) gets the very same setup as `.fearless`, and is checked like it. These extensions are invented by Fearless, so on a machine where one of them is set up in any other way than the manager's own, the setup was tampered with: a captured one lost is a fatal error, and a new one refused fails the compile.
- A system extension gets the same setup as `.fearless` too, but the manager takes it over from whatever program opens it: the user allowed it on purpose, so other programs registered for it do not refuse it. A Windows UserChoice still does, as for `.fearless`: the user pinned that extension to a program, and the manager does not undo the user's own choice. When an extension leaves `systemExtensions`, the manager removes its own setup only, and the previous program opens those files again.

The portable Fearless reconciles `.fearless` under its own identity at every start, removing the manager's associations, and a running manager quits. That is accepted: at its next start the manager takes back every extension it manages.

#### Extensions on Linux

On Linux a program opens MIME types, not extensions; the shared MIME database turns a file name into a type through name patterns (globs). Several extensions can share one type (`*.htm` and `*.html` are both `text/html`), so taking over a type would take over all its extensions. The manager never does that: it gives each claimed extension a type of its own, in its own MIME package (the file `$XDG_DATA_HOME/mime/packages/<identity>.xml`, which only the manager writes):
```
<mime-type type="application/x-fearless-htm">
  <glob pattern="*.htm" weight="100"/>
  <sub-class-of type="text/html"/>
  <sub-class-of type="application/xhtml+xml"/>
</mime-type>
```
- The glob weight is 100, above every glob shipped by the systems we target (at most 80), so the name `x.htm` turns into the manager's type and `x.html` stays `text/html`.
- When a system glob also matches the name, GIO and Qt look at the file content to choose, and text content would go to the system type. The `sub-class-of` entries, one for each type the system database gives to `*.htm`, make the content of those types count as the manager's type. A file whose content is unmistakably another format (a `.htm` file starting with `<?php`) still gets that other type; this is accepted.
- The manager's `.desktop` lists the manager's types; no other program lists them, so a double click on a `.htm` file reaches the manager, and every other program opening `text/html` stays in the "open with" choices.
- `.fearless` keeps its type `application/x-fearless`. A Fearless extension gets `application/x-fearless-<ext>` like a system extension; no system glob can match it.
- Nothing outside the manager's own files changes. Releasing an extension rewrites the manager's package without it, and the system types are back as they were.
- The Linux counterpart of the Windows UserChoice is a `[Default Applications]` entry, in any `mimeapps.list`, naming another program for the manager's type: it refuses the extension.

#### Icons on the desktop

On Windows the registry names an icon by its path, so the desktop reads the `.ico` straight from `.fearless_out/icons/`. On Linux a MIME type names its icon, and the desktop looks names up only in icon theme folders: the user's `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons`, the system's `$XDG_DATA_DIRS/icons` (root only) and the legacy `~/.icons`. `reconcile` installs each icon as `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons/hicolor/256x256/mimetypes/<identity>-<hash>.png`; the hash gives a changed image a new name, past the icon cache of the desktop. Every file there named `<identity>-<hash>.png` belongs to that identity: each `reconcile` deletes those not in the wanted set, and removing an identity deletes all of them together with its `apps/<identity>.png`, its `.desktop` file and its MIME package.

### Opening a file

The desktop starts the manager program with the path of the double clicked file as its only argument; for a file that is not a `.fearless` file the message is `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>`. The single manager reacts:

1. One main claims the extension: run that main, as `Run <name> <main>` does: the project is selected, only that main runs, and it becomes the selected main of the project. The running main receives the file as its first input file (see Input files).
2. Several mains claim it: a small window lists them with their icons as `project::pkg.Main`, sorted. Picking an entry sends `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`, with the project and the main of that entry; that message runs the main as in 1. Closing the window runs nothing.
3. No main claims the extension (an extension of `systemExtensions` that no main opens): the message is refused, saying that no main opens files with that extension.

An empty file runs nothing: the manager shows a note naming the file. GNOME types every empty file as `application/x-zerosize` whatever its name and never hands it to the manager; refusing empty files everywhere makes Windows and the other desktops behave the same.

### Input files

A running main sees its input files through `System.inputCursor` of the standard library: the list of the files given to the program, in the order they arrived.
The list starts with the file opened by `OpenWithOrShortcut`, a document or a shortcut file alike, and starts empty for a main started by `Run`.
Dropping files on a window of a running Fearless program adds them at the end of the list, in the order of the drop. A drop holding anything that is not a file, a folder for example, adds nothing and is refused.
This holds for the portable Fearless too: dropping files on a window is part of the runtime of every Fearless program.
`sys.inputCursor` is a cursor on the first position of the list; `#` gives the file at that position, empty until a file arrives there, and `.next` the cursor on the next position. So a program sees a new file by asking again at the first position that was empty.
An input file offers the reading methods of the autoloaded `TxtFile` and `ImageFile` assets, without their `AssetReadCapability` argument: the input file is itself the capability. Every read reads the file again, and a file that can not be read any more is a non deterministic error.

### Messages from a running main

A running main sends messages to the manager through `System.manager` of the standard library, the capability `CapManager`.
Each method of `CapManager` is one verb, and gives an action.
The `.run` of that action does what Eclipse does: it listens on a port, runs the manager program with `FearlessCommand<Verb>`, the arguments of the verb and that port, and ends when the manager ends the connection.
So the `.run` of an action ends once the manager has applied its message: for `Run` and `Compile`, once the job ends.
The action of `Run` and `Compile` gives the Output of the job, all the text the manager sent on the port; the other actions give `Void`.
A kind is a method of its own (`.kindCode`), and so is a link for reading or for writing (`.linkRead`, `.linkWrite`): a message of a running main can not hold a kind that is not one of the four, or a link that is neither `read` nor `write`.
A path is the list of the bytes naming it, the bytes of its `Base16:` form. A path that is not absolute is refused by the running main itself: no message is sent, and the action gives an Info.
The manager shows a refused message in a dialog, as for every other message, and the action gives an Info saying why, the text after the line `Refused`.
The Output that the action of `Run` and `Compile` gives is the text after the line `Applied`.
`Quit`, `Reset` and `Terminate` of the project of the running main end the running main before the `.run` of their action ends.
A program started by the portable Fearless has no manager: every action of `CapManager` gives an Info.

### System extensions

The system extensions the user allowed are `systemExtensions` in `projects.info`, changed like the rest of `projects.info`. Removing an extension from `systemExtensions` makes the manager stop capturing it, and deletes the compiled cache of every project whose mains open it: those projects need compiling again.

When a compile wants a system extension that is not in `systemExtensions`, the compile fails, naming the main, the claim and the extension. The window then asks in a dialog whether to add the extension to `systemExtensions`: yes is an `EditMetadata` adding it, and the project needs compiling again. Adding an extension to `systemExtensions` captures it at once; when reconcile refuses it (a Windows UserChoice, for example), the edit is invalid.

### Conflict icon

The Fearless icon with a purple `?` on top of it, stored as a PNG in the standard library at `base/icons/conflict.png`. The manager reads it from the stdLib folder of its application image and materialises it like any other icon, the PNG and its `.ico`, in the `icons` folder of the manager folder.

It is the autoloaded type `base.IconsConflict`. `base` autoloads its assets as if its files were under a `_base` folder: `.path` is `fear:/_base/icons/conflict.png` and `.diskPath` is `icons/conflict.png`, relative to the stdLib `base` folder, which a running main receives as `fearlessBase.dir`.

## Eclipse

Connect Eclipse asks for Eclipse: its program, a file next to it, its folder, or the folder Eclipse was unzipped into. The Eclipse folder is the one folder holding `.eclipseproduct`.
The connect eclipse operation uses a file picker and do some heuristics to find the eclipse folder from the chosen file.
The accepted cases are as follows:
-we select a file that is the eclipse folder.
-we select a file that is directly inside the eclipse folder.
-we select a file whose direct parent folder is inside the eclipse folder.
-we select a folder that contains only the eclipse folder.
Connect Eclipse puts the plugin in the Eclipse folder, with `manager.info` next to it: the path of the manager program folder. From that path the plugin finds the manager program, the manager folder and the compiled standard library.

Plugin design: Eclipse reads `projects.info` and follows up into the `.fearless_out` files when needed to recover information and display the state.
The Output of a running program has no bound on its size, so the manager does not pass it through files: Eclipse listens on a port, and its `Run` and `Compile` carry that port.
Eclipse shows a refused `Run` or `Compile` only through the dialog of the manager: the Output view stays empty.
Eclipse follows exactly one job: the job of its last `Run` or `Compile`; Eclipse sends no other `Run` or `Compile` until that job ends, except for a save. Everything else Eclipse shows (the error of the last compile, the unit test log) is read from files.

Eclipse commands the manager by running the manager program with the arguments `FearlessCommand<Verb>` and the arguments of the verb. If a message ends up in error, the manager shows the error as usual.

Saving a file of a code project in Eclipse compiles the project. Users save often, and a compile near its end should not be thrown away:
- if the followed job is a compile of that project that started more than 15 seconds ago, the save is ignored: the files stay unsaved in their editors;
- otherwise Eclipse sends `Terminate` of the project (a run is always ended, and so is a younger compile), saves every open file of the project together, and sends `Compile` of the project.

The plugin mirrors every registered project into the Eclipse workspace, in the Fearless perspective only, and writes into a project folder only the files and folders the user saves or creates; a project the manager forgets is removed from the workspace. A workspace project named like a registered project that is not its mirror is an error. Every two seconds it reflects `projects.info` and the `.fearless_out` folders: the error of the last compile (`compile_error.txt`) in the Problems view, on its file at its line, or on the project while that file does not exist, and the error of the last check (`check_error.txt`) on the project, each while it is not out of date, and in the JUnit view the unit test log the running main writes in `.out/logs`: its complete records, its lines up to the last newline, without a test case whose closing line is not written yet. It shows the followed job in the Output view, emptied when a job starts, like the Output area of the manager: a view with no buttons, where a stack frame and the header of a compile error are links opening that file at that line. Fearless files show with syntax highlighting. Build (`Compile` for a code project with valid content, `Check` for any other), Clean (`DeleteCache`), the main button below, the kind switches of the Fearless menu of a project, New Project (`Register`), Show in manager (`Show` with the name of the project) and Forget project are messages to the manager.
The toolbar of the Fearless perspective holds the main button of the manager for the project of the active editor or of the selection. It shows what pressing it does, each with an image of its own: Check, Compile, Run, Run V, or Terminate while Eclipse follows a job of that project. Run V asks which mains to run, with the selected mains checked. Besides it, the toolbar of the Fearless perspective holds only the buttons of text editing (Save, Undo, Redo, Search, the toggles of the editor and the navigation in the text); a button that does nothing for Fearless or repeats another is hidden, and so is the Run menu, in the Fearless perspective only. Eclipse finds the state from the files, as the manager does: the kind and the links in `projects.info`, the marker, the packages and their rank files, and whether compiling would compile some package. A project whose content is invalid in another way shows Compile, and the manager refuses that `Compile`, saying why.
The toolbar of the Fearless perspective also holds New Package and New Type, for the project of the active editor or of the selection; each asks for a path inside the project folder and refuses a path that is not valid, saying why:
- New Package takes a path like `lib/_beer`, and makes the package folder `_beer` with the folders holding it;
- New Type takes a path like `lib/_beer/point`, and makes the file `point.fear` holding the type `Point`. Rank file makes the rank file of that package instead, `_rank_app.fear` or the rank chosen, holding the usual `use` lines of the standard library. Example tests adds a test suite checking some arithmetic: a project runs its tests more than a bare main.
New Folder and New File of the Fearless perspective warn when the name is not a valid name of a Fearless project, saying why, and create it anyway.
New Fearless Project registers a folder, made if missing (`Register`). It can first write a demo into a new or empty folder, then make the project code (`Kind`) once the manager lists it: Hello world; Simple GUI, a window with a button; Shortcut and OpenWith, a main started by its shortcut file and a main opening the files of its extension, each with its icon. The OpenWith main of this demo is a window showing the text of the file it is opened with; every file dropped on that window adds its text at the end.
A missing or malformed file of the manager is an error shown in Eclipse, and the plugin stops following the manager until Eclipse starts again.

## Content assist

Content assist works in a Fearless file of a package folder of a mirrored project, after a dot and on request. It uses the compiled information of the last compile of the project and of the compiled standard library: the api json of every package, the map directives of the project as that compile resolved them, and the text documentation of the package of each proposal. A malformed api json or map, a missing documentation file, and a public type or a method of a type missing from the documentation are errors.

It proposes:
- nothing while the cursor is inside a comment or a string, or touches a name (`ps|`, `Perso|`);
- after a dot, or after a space following an expression, the methods of the type of the expression before the cursor, dot methods and operators alike: dot methods first, then by name and number of parameters; every method is proposed, also several with the same name and number of parameters (methods differing in the reference capability of the receiver). A method name being typed (`.fl|`, `+|`) keeps the methods starting with it and is replaced by the choice; otherwise the choice replaces the dot, or is inserted at the cursor. An operator is inserted with a space on each side;
- after a lowercase name and a dot (`base.|`), and inside a qualified type name being typed (`base.Li|`, in code or in a `use` directive), the public types of the compiled package that name stands for whose simple name starts with what follows the dot.

Each proposal shows its signature, with simple type names and with reference capabilities, and its documentation.

The text is read with the tokens of the compiler. The expression before the cursor is what follows the last `;`, `,`, `->`, `:` or `'` in the innermost bracket around the cursor, read with the grammar of the compiler: an atom, then posts left to right, where a post without parenthesis takes one atom, and `.m x = e rest` binds `x`. A bracket not closed yet runs to the end of the text. What the compiler would reject is unknown, and unknown has no methods. The type of:
- a type name is that type: a simple name is the alias of a `use` directive of the head file of the package (its only `_rank_` file), else a type of the package; a name without `[..]` has no generics. A package name written in the package, in a qualified name or a `use` directive, stands for the package the `map` directives of the project give it there. A number or a string is a `base.Nat`, `base.Int`, `base.Float` or `base.Str`;
- a name is the type of what binds it. The literals around the cursor bind their self name, or `this` for a top level declaration, the generics of a declaration and of the method holding the cursor, and the parameters of that method: to their written type, else to the type of the parameter of the method the literal implements (the method of that name, or, for a method without name, the only abstract method with that number of parameters not implemented by name in the literal). An earlier `x = e` of the same expression binds `x` to the first parameter of the continuation of its method; inside its own `e`, `x` is unknown;
- a literal is the type it declares, or the type written before it, or else the type expected by the call it is an argument of, or the result type of the method it is the whole body of; the generics of that type still open are bound by the types of the bodies of its methods;
- a call is the result types of every method of that name and number of arguments of the type of its receiver (several when they differ in the reference capability of the receiver, or when the supertypes of a type the last compile does not know disagree), each with the generics of its method bound by the explicit type arguments, then by each argument meeting the type of its parameter, left to right, then by the type expected of the call. An expression of several types has the methods of all of them.

A type declared in the edited file that the last compile does not know has every method of its supertypes: when supertypes have methods with the same name and number of parameters, all of them are proposed.

## Error management
Errors are present in Coordinator (`Report`, `Violation`) when shared with portableFearless, and in Controllers when unique to the manager.
Controllers must have a similar (`Report`, `Violation`) classification inside it.

## Future design

What the design will cover later; nothing in this section is current behaviour.
- The Output keeps only the last part of what a job prints, so a program printing without end does not fill the memory.
- The GUI becomes a separate program, as for many Linux daemons: the manager has no window, and the GUI commands the single manager through the manager program with `FearlessCommand<Verb>` arguments, as every other program does. The GUI could be a Fearless program with some extra permissions.
- A later double click on a file for a main already running adds that file to the input files of the running main.
- A double click for a main already running signals the running main to wake up, for example bringing its window to the front.
- A captured extension that no main claims gets its own icon, showing that the manager holds it, instead of the conflict icon.
- The fields of the Links area show what they take and complete what is typed: the type names the code uses for that data project, separated by spaces.
- Removing or changing a data project that a code project links shows a confirmation dialog in the GUI, naming the code projects that become invalid.
- A data project folder can be deleted when it is left empty, and so can a zip, since a zip is a folder for a main. Deleting something and writing it again "the same" can then give another structure on disk: what was a zip can come back as a plain folder. How to handle this is to be discussed.
- A main learns the extensions it claims, for example through a `System` method. A main with `OpenWith[I]` gets an extension such as `ffile042`, chosen by the manager and varying between machines and compile orders; to save a document the user can later double click, the program must name it `<something>.ffile042`.

## Implementation notes

What a rewrite needs that neither this design nor the other repositories say.

An implementation decides alone every detail the design leaves open below its concepts: wire formats, encodings, intervals, the library calls, the content of the files the design names (information for Eclipse can be added to `manager.info`). It never adds what the user or another program can observe and the design does not describe: a file in the manager folder or in a project, a verb, a GUI element, a message, a fallback. Those go into the design first.

- A field of the Links area sends its edit, a `Link`, on the first of: a click anywhere outside the field, the field losing the keyboard focus, Enter, Tab, Esc, a typed character that no type name holds (that character is not added), and one second with no key typed.
- The stand-in for the tray: under Wayland a program can not place its windows, but AWT on Linux is an X11 client (through XWayland under Wayland), so the manager places the window itself.
  The stand-in is an AWT window of type popup. On X11 the window manager does not manage a popup window: no taskbar lists it, and it stays where the manager places it.
- A double click on a button presses it twice, and the second press comes before the request of the first changes the window, so it sends the same request again. Every button is disabled for half a second after it is pressed.
- Messages as files: only runs of the manager program write message files, so a file is not the only possible way for a run to reach the single manager. Another channel, for example a local socket of the single manager, would not depend on the file system reporting new files. Replacing the message files with another channel is under consideration.
- The single manager: the process holding the lock (`FileChannel.tryLock`) of the file `instance.lock` in the manager folder. The operating system releases the lock when the process ends, however it ends, so a manager that was killed leaves no stale lock.

- Tray on Linux: `java.awt.SystemTray` needs an XEmbed tray, which GNOME and most Wayland desktops do not have; what they show is a StatusNotifierItem on the session bus, registered with `org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher`, its menu served as `com.canonical.dbusmenu`. A desktop with no owner of the watcher name may still have an XEmbed tray (MATE, i3): there the manager uses `java.awt.SystemTray`, as on Windows and macOS. A desktop with neither has no tray. The JDK has no D-Bus client, so the manager speaks the D-Bus wire protocol itself on the `unix:path=` socket of `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`.
- Clicks on the tray icon on Linux: each desktop decides what a click on a StatusNotifierItem does.
  Some desktops show the menu served as `com.canonical.dbusmenu` themselves.
  Other desktops report the click to the item: `Activate` (usually a left click), `SecondaryActivate` (usually a middle click) or `ContextMenu` (usually a right click), each with the point of the click.
  The manager answers each of these three by opening its own menu at that point, with the same entries.
  So the click opening the menu may differ from one desktop to another (left or right, single or double), and the menu may look different for different clicks on the same desktop; every one of those clicks opens the menu.
- The bytes of a `Base16:` path: on Windows the UTF-16 code units of the name, little endian (a name may hold an unpaired surrogate); elsewhere the bytes `sun.jnu.encoding` gives, the bytes the JVM turns names into.
- Output links in Eclipse: a stack frame of the runtime reads `error line: N in file F`; a compile error reads `In file: fear:/F` and, on a later line, the numbered source line `N| ` with a line of `^` under it (the lines before it are context). `F` is relative to the project folder. The Output view fills in by pieces, so the line of a compile error is looked up when the link is used. The Output view shows a `TextConsole` in a `TextConsoleViewer`, and links text through an `IPatternMatchListener` of that console.
- Hidden buttons in Eclipse: the extension point `org.eclipse.ui.perspectiveExtensions` hides toolbar items (`hiddenToolBarItem`) and menus (`hiddenMenuItem`) by id in one perspective. The ids are those of the buttons the Eclipse IDE package shows; Window > Perspective > Customize Perspective hides items by hand, and the workbench model saved at exit lists their ids.
- Syntax highlighting in Eclipse: `org.eclipse.tm4e` colours a file from a TextMate grammar. The only grammar of Fearless is `syntaxes/fearless.tmLanguage.json` of the plugin, with the themes `fearless-light.css` and `fearless-dark.css`; a rewrite keeps those files, or writes the grammar again from the tokens of the Frontend.
- JUnit view in Eclipse: `JUnitCore.importTestRunSession` of `org.eclipse.jdt.junit.core` shows a JUnit XML report file.
- Port of a message: a TCP port on the loopback address. The manager connects, sends the status line, then the reason of a refusal or the Output of a job, as UTF-8 text, then closes the connection. Eclipse and a running main listen on a port the system picks.
